

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — a known group of 5–50 means we can skip the account-lifecycle machinery and keep the foundation small.

  Now the stack. Noting from your CLAUDE.md that you have a deep Python toolchain set up (micromamba + uv, ruff/mypy
  conventions, RST docstrings) and no JS tooling configured at all — so I'm reading you as a Python-first maintainer
  unless you say otherwise.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the browser or the server owns the UI. A server-rendered app sends HTML per request; the
  notification work then lives entirely server-side, which is where it has to be anyway (a change by person A must reach
  person B, whose browser isn't running). A SPA + JSON API splits that — you get a nicer live-updating feed, but you
  now maintain two codebases, a build step, and an auth story that works for both. For 5–50 internal users, the SPA
  mostly buys polish you'd pay for in maintenance.

  What's hard to change later is the API boundary: if we go server-rendered and later want a mobile client, the JSON API
  has to be added. What's cheap is sprinkling in a bit of JavaScript for live updates — server-rendered doesn't lock
  you out of that.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m server-rendered Python (FastAPI or Flask + templates) with SQLite, and a small amount of vanilla JS
  for the in-app notification badge. Boring, one codebase, one deployment, and it puts the change-detection logic in
  the one place that can actually see changes.
